{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Live memory + MCP health, verified against AWS tonight (read-only)\n\n**MCP endpoint is healthy right now; the 502 is NOT currently reproducible.**\nRead-only probes (no gate started, nothing billable):\n- `GET /mcp` -> **401** in 0.27s. 401 is the CORRECT healthy code per observability.sh §1b: only\n  the app's auth middleware can produce it, so it proves the whole TLS -> nginx -> upstream -> app\n  path.\n- `POST /mcp` JSON-RPC initialize (unauth) -> 401. `GET /review/health` -> 200.\n  `GET /config/server/version` -> 200, Gerrit 3.14.1. TLS valid to 2026-11-27.\n- `rebar/host:mcp_healthy` = **1.0 on all 259 datapoints over 24h — zero unhealthy ticks.**\n- Alarm `rebar-mcp-serving-path-down` is live, `treat_missing_data=breaching`, OK.\n\nNo attempt was made to reproduce by starting an async gate: the confirmed root cause is a kernel\nOOM-kill of the MCP container mid-gate, and reproducing it requires real concurrent gate load.\nSo \"not reproducible right now\" is NOT \"structurally prevented\".\n\n## AC5 data: live memory numbers\n\n`mem_available_percent` ranged **27-60%** over 3 days, with `mem_probe_ok`=1 throughout — so\nthese are measured, not defaulted. 27% available on 8 GiB is roughly **2.2 GiB free**.\n\nThat is consistent with the oversubscription arithmetic already on this ticket (~10 concurrent\nreviews x ~739 MB + ~2.17 GB steady state ~= 9.6 GB demand against an 8 GiB host). It does not\nclose AC5 — still missing steady-state PER-CONTAINER RSS and the RSS-vs-ticket-size relationship,\nboth of which need on-box data this read-only sweep could not gather.\n\n## A gap worth naming: there is still NO memory alarm\n\n`mem_available_percent` is **measurement-only**. Nothing pages on it. Given that the confirmed\nroot cause of this bug is an OOM kill, and given the host runs at 27-60% available with a\ndemand model that exceeds capacity, the absence of a memory alarm means the next OOM will again\nbe discovered by its consequences rather than announced.\n\nTwo live corroborating signals: `deploy_deferrals` ran to **69 in 24h** — the autodeploy path\ncorrectly declining to kill in-flight reviews, healthy behaviour but strong contention evidence;\nand `rebar-autodeploy-mcp-mem-abort` exists and is correctly `breaching`, which shows the memory\nDIMENSION is already considered alarm-worthy elsewhere in the stack.\n\n## The nginx failover gap is deferred, not fixed\n\nRecording it here so a future reader does not mistake current health for resolution: nginx's\n`upstream rebar_mcp` has a single `server` with no failover, so a recurrence of the OOM would\nstill 502 until a redeploy. The difference from 2026-09-02 is detection latency, not prevention —\n`rebar-mcp-serving-path-down` would now page within ~15 minutes instead of a human noticing 12\nhours later. AC1 (detection) and AC3 (retire sweep) are live and verified; the underlying memory\noversubscription is explicitly deferred pending AC5.\n\n## Unrelated durability observation\n\nThere is **no CloudWatch log group for the rebar Gerrit host** — its logs live only in on-box\njournald and are lost with the host. During the 09-02 outage there was consequently no off-box\nlog record. Not an error and not in this ticket's scope, but it is the reason post-incident\nforensics were limited to what the host still held."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788546380501981001,"uuid":"1b3963c9-f510-4899-8cdf-7415ccc3e29d"}